! MCP's HOLLERITH CHANNEL-NUMBER CONVERSION, printed p.17 lines
! 32-36, lifted out of the page and run on its own.
!
! The four rows, as the listing prints them:
!
!   X132    CT0011(V+I)(BU,7), .12($4)   -TEST TENS POSITION OF
!                                         CHANNEL
!           LCV(DU,8,4), $LZC+.3, 46     -CONVERT TO OCTAL NUMBER
!   X132AA  CT0011(V+I)(BU,16), .22($4)  -TEST UNITS POSITION
!           +(BU,4), $LZC+.3, 46         -FORM CHANNEL NUMBER
!
! WHAT IT IS DOING, which took three separate pieces of machinery to
! make runnable and is worth stating once in one place.
!
! A HOLLERITH DIGIT IS A POSITION, NOT A VALUE: one punch among
! several rows of a card column.  CONNECT FOR TEST discards its
! result and sets only the counts, so running it over the column
! leaves the number of zeros to the left of the punch -- THE DIGIT
! ITSELF -- in the left-zeros counter at word 7 bits 17-23
! (Reference Manual printed pp.75-76).  "$LZC+.3" is word 7 bit 20,
! so an eight-bit field there is the counter's low nibble followed by
! four bits of the word beyond it, which are zero: the tens digit
! over a zero.  LOAD CONVERTED reads that as two decimal digits
! (pp.71-73) and leaves tens*10 in the accumulator at offset 46.
! The second CONNECT FOR TEST puts the units digit in the counter,
! and "+(BU,4)" adds the four-bit counter field at the same offset.
!
! Channel number = tens*10 + units, built out of two punch POSITIONS
! by a counter, a radix conversion and an add.
!
! THREE THINGS HAD TO EXIST FOR THIS TO RUN, all built 2026-10-19/20:
! KF (which decides the card is an IOCD card, two rows earlier), LCV,
! and the connective counts.  None of them was reachable before, and
! each on its own was not enough -- building LCV without the counters
! converts nothing, because the counter is where its input comes
! from.
!
! WHAT IS CHANGED FROM THE PAGE, and it is only the addressing: the
! listing reads its columns through progressive indexing off $4,
! which is walking a card image, and this deck reads two fixed
! columns instead.  The connectives, the conversion, the offsets and
! the add are the page's own.
!
! THE COLUMNS.  A tens digit of 3 is a punch with three zeros to its
! left in a seven-bit column; a units digit of 5 is a punch with five
! zeros to its left in the sixteen-bit column the page uses for the
! units position.  So the channel number comes out 35.
!
!   T1  after the LOAD CONVERTED: 30, at offset 46.
!   T2  after the add: 35, at offset 46.  **T1 is checked as well as
!       T2 because the two halves fail differently** -- a broken
!       counter gives the wrong tens AND the wrong units, while a
!       broken conversion leaves T1 wrong and T2 wrong by the same
!       amount.  Seeing both says which half moved.

! ONE INJECTION, MEASURED: LOAD CONVERTED made to copy the digit bits
! instead of converting them reads the eight-bit field 0x30 as binary
! 48 rather than decimal 30, giving T1 = 0xc000000000000 and
! T2 = 0xd400000000000 -- the channel number comes out 53 instead of
! 35.  Both checks move, by the same arithmetic, which is what tells
! the conversion apart from the counter underneath it.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

START   CT0011(BU,7), TENS+.57, 0     ! -TEST TENS POSITION OF CHANNEL
        LCV(DU,8,4), $LZC+.3, 46      ! -CONVERT TO OCTAL NUMBER
        ST(BU,64,8), T1, 0            ! (not in the page: look at it)
        CT0011(BU,16), UNITS+.48, 0   ! -TEST UNITS POSITION
        +(BU,4), $LZC+.3, 46          ! -FORM CHANNEL NUMBER
        ST(BU,64,8), T2, 0

PASS    BEW, $

! Data, word-aligned: BEW is a half word, so without this the 64-bit
! fields below would straddle two words.
        SLC, (8)2020.0

! A seven-bit column with three zeros to the left of the punch.
TENS    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000010
! A sixteen-bit column with five zeros to the left of the punch.
UNITS   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000002000

T1      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T2      DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, START
